---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Callout } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const FAQ_ITEMS = [
  {
    title: 'What is OpenFlows, and how does it relate to Coder?',
    content: `
      <p>OpenFlows is an autonomous AI software team that turns GitHub issues into reviewed,
      pull requests. Manager owns organization and tenant state, workspace lifecycle, runtime
      identity, and credential brokerage. Coder hosts the isolated workspaces where agents run.
      Nexus coordinates planning, review, and delivery inside a tenant.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'Which models can OpenFlows use?',
    content: `
      <p>Models are enabled in the platform&rsquo;s AI settings (the Coder dashboard), and you then
      assign one to each role in the agent registry. Every AI call happens centrally through your
      own model gateway in the control plane, so you choose the models available to your team and
      can switch a role to a different model live, without a restart.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'Where do AI calls happen?',
    content: `
      <p>Model access follows the configured Coder and agent runtime settings. Manager brokers
      infrastructure credentials; it does not imply that workspaces never handle secrets. A
      workspace can receive a scoped bootstrap bundle and short-lived GitHub installation token.
      See the <a href="/docs/architecture/credentials">credential flow</a>.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'How does an agent act inside a workspace?',
    content: `
      <p>Each agent runs in its own isolated, disposable workspace provisioned from Coder. It
      registers with Manager, uses tenant-scoped runtime credentials, and exchanges task messages
      with Nexus through the hook and A2A path. Its authority is bounded by tenant, workspace,
      repository, and purpose.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'Can I run OpenFlows on a private repository?',
    content: `
      <p>Yes, when the GitHub App installation has access to that repository. Manager verifies
      the repository belongs to the tenant's organization and brokers short-lived, scoped
      installation tokens. Human GitHub OAuth is used for sign-in, not as the runtime Git token.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'Can I run OpenFlows air-gapped or on-premises?',
    content: `
      <p>You can self-host Manager, PostgreSQL, Redis, and Coder in your infrastructure. The
      current GitHub integration needs network access to GitHub for OAuth, App installation,
      webhooks, and API calls, so a fully air-gapped deployment is not supported as described.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'How does SENTINEL decide whether to approve or reject work?',
    content: `
      <p>SENTINEL is instructed to attack the work for security holes, quality issues, and missing
      test coverage against the agreed plan. It reviews from its own isolated workspace and never
      touches the builder&rsquo;s files. When it needs proof, it requests a safe, allowlisted command
      run for evidence. The posture is straightforward: when in doubt, don&rsquo;t approve.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'What happens if the work gets stuck or times out?',
    content: `
      <p>Recovery is built into the system. A central reconciliation loop retries failing work a
      bounded number of times. When retries are exhausted, the ticket is parked and escalated to a
      human  -  with a notification and links  -  rather than failing silently or spinning forever.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'How much does OpenFlows cost to run?',
    content: `
      <p>OpenFlows itself is open-source and free to run; the cost is your own model usage through
      your gateway. You control it by choosing a model suited to each role  -  for example, a more
      careful, higher-cost model for the reviewer and a general coding model for the builder  -  and
      tuning from there.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'Can I run multiple workers for a role in parallel?',
    content: `
      <p>Yes. The agent registry sets how many workers a role may run at once (its concurrency
      ceiling). OpenFlows runs a fleet of worker workspaces in parallel and assigns work to
      whichever is free.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'Is my code sent to third parties?',
    content: `
      <p>Code is available to the workspace and configured model provider. GitHub also receives
      repository API and Git traffic. Review the provider, Coder, and GitHub data paths before
      deploying sensitive repositories.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'What happens when there are merge conflicts?',
    content: `
      <p>VESSEL, the DevOps role, watches CI and resolves merge conflicts. If a merge block is
      genuinely unresolvable, the work escalates to a human rather than being guessed at. Approved
      pull requests are merged when green.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'What happens to workspaces after a task completes?',
    content: `
      <p>Worker workspaces are short-lived and disposable. They are provisioned when work is
      assigned and can be torn down when no longer needed. Tokens expire or rotate, but
      workspace teardown and credential revocation should be verified in operations.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'What is the coordination store?',
    content: `
      <p>The coordination store is the single, durable record of truth for the whole team  -  every
      ticket, worker, approval, review, and pull request. Only the orchestrator and the worker
      command surface may write to it, and writes are validated. Because the whole team reads one
      consistent store, Manager can recover queued work after a process failure. Tenant isolation
      still depends on correct runtime identity and authorization checks.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'How does the system recover from failures?',
    content: `
      <p>A central reconciliation loop re-reads the whole system on a steady cadence, advances
      every piece of work, and repairs what is broken  -  with bounded retries. When it hits
      something that needs a human decision, it stops and escalates to you rather than guessing.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'How do I change the configuration or extend a role?',
    content: `
      <p>Add a skill, connect an MCP server, or enable a different model as a configuration-only
      change through the control panel  -  no restart or redeploy. Adding a brand-new role is the one
      extension that touches the system&rsquo;s internals. See the Guides and the Agent Registry
      concept page.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'Where is the API reference?',
    content: `
      <p>OpenFlows is driven through the <a href="/docs/reference/command-surface">Command Surface</a>
      reference, which covers the control panel and its command-line equivalent in plain language.
      There is no separate public HTTP API documented here.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    title: 'Where do I go if something is broken?',
    content: `
      <p>Start with the <a href="/docs/guides/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> guide. It covers
      bootstrap failures, Coder connectivity, stuck tickets, and merge conflicts. If you cannot
      resolve the issue, open a report on the OpenFlows GitHub repository.</p>
    `,
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Resources', href: '/docs/resources' }, { label: 'FAQ' }]}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <p>
    Common questions about OpenFlows architecture, security, and operations. For step-by-step diagnostics,
    see the <a href="/docs/guides/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> guide. Cannot find what you are
    looking for?
    <a href="https://github.com/The-AgenticFlow/OpenFlows/discussions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ask on GitHub Discussions</a>
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Key architecture facts">
    <ul>
      <li>Coder decides <em>where</em> work runs; OpenFlows decides <em>how</em> agents coordinate.</li>
      <li>Every AI call happens through your own model gateway in the control plane.</li>
      <li>Manager owns long-lived operator and GitHub App secrets; workspaces receive scoped runtime material.</li>
      <li>GitHub OAuth signs in humans; GitHub App installation tokens authorize runtime repository access.</li>
      <li>Config and extension happen through the control panel, no restart required.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <div class="accordion">
    {FAQ_ITEMS.map((item, i) => (
      <div class="accordionItem">
        <div class="accordionHeader" set:html={`data-index="${i}"`}>
          <span class="accordionTitle">{item.title}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="accordionBody" set:html={item.content} />
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.accordionHeader').forEach(header => {
    header.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const item = header.closest('.accordionItem');
      const isOpen = item.classList.contains('open');

      document.querySelectorAll('.accordionItem.open').forEach(openItem => {
        openItem.classList.remove('open');
      });

      if (!isOpen) {
        item.classList.add('open');
      }
    });
  });
</script>
